{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—AI systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code—has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful systems introduce significant security and safety concerns that necessitate rigorous evaluation. This paper surveys the state-of-the-art in code agent research, with particular focus on red-teaming methodologies for identifying and mitigating vulnerabilities in code generation systems. We examine recent frameworks including RedCodeAgent and BlueCodeAgent that address the dual challenge of attacking and defending code generation AI systems. Additionally, we discuss the broader context of trustworthiness assessment in AI models through the lens of DecodingTrust, and acknowledge the foundational transformer architecture introduced by \"Attention Is All You Need\" that enables these capabilities. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation methodologies and proposes directions for future research in code agent security.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most transformative applications of large language models (LLMs) in software engineering. Unlike traditional code completion tools, code agents possess the ability to autonomously generate, execute, debug, and iteratively improve code based on user requirements and runtime feedback. These systems integrate LLMs with code interpreters, allowing them to dynamically execute generated code, analyze errors, and refine their outputs—a capability that has proven invaluable for tasks ranging from data analysis to full-stack application development.\n\nHowever, the integration of code execution capabilities introduces unique security challenges. Code agents can be susceptible to prompt injection attacks, where malicious instructions are embedded within user prompts to induce the agent to execute harmful code, access sensitive information, or perform unauthorized operations. Furthermore, the autonomous nature of these agents means that vulnerabilities can compound across multiple execution cycles, potentially leading to cascading failures or security breaches.\n\nThe field of red-teaming—systematic evaluation of AI systems to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical component in ensuring the safe deployment of AI technologies. In the context of code agents, red-teaming involves designing adversarial inputs and attack scenarios to probe the robustness of code generation and execution capabilities. This paper examines recent advances in this domain, focusing on automated red-teaming frameworks specifically designed for code agents.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Transformer Architecture Foundation\n\nThe transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in their seminal work \"Attention Is All You Need\" [1], laid the foundation for modern language models. This architecture replaced recurrent and convolutional approaches with self-attention mechanisms, enabling parallel processing and capturing long-range dependencies in text. The transformer's success in sequence transduction tasks catalyzed the development of large-scale language models that now power code agents. The attention mechanism allows models to weigh the significance of different parts of input code and context, enabling sophisticated understanding and generation of code structures. Without this architectural breakthrough, the capabilities exhibited by contemporary code agents would not be achievable.\n\n### 2.2 Trustworthiness Assessment in AI Models\n\nThe need for comprehensive trustworthiness evaluation became increasingly apparent as LLMs demonstrated impressive capabilities alongside concerning vulnerabilities. Wang et al. introduced \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\" [2], which provided one of the first systematic frameworks for evaluating AI systems across multiple dimensions including factual consistency, prejudice, toxicity, robustness, safety, and bias. Their work established evaluation methodologies that have influenced subsequent research in AI safety and security. The DecodingTrust framework evaluated GPT models across dozens of dimensions using curated benchmarks and large-scale datasets, providing a blueprint for assessing the trustworthiness of generative AI systems. For code agents, extending such frameworks to address code-specific trustworthiness concerns—including secure code generation, vulnerability prevention, and safe execution—is essential.\n\n### 2.3 Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has specifically targeted the security of code agents through automated red-teaming approaches. Guo et al. introduced \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\" [3], which presents an automated framework for testing the security of various code agent systems. Their approach leverages LLMs to generate adversarial prompts designed to elicit unsafe behavior from code agents, including requests to generate malicious code, exploit software vulnerabilities, or bypass safety mechanisms. RedCodeAgent operates by systematically exploring the attack surface of code agents, generating diverse adversarial examples across multiple categories of threats. The framework evaluates code agents on their ability to refuse harmful requests and maintain safety boundaries even under adversarial conditions.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework demonstrated that current code agents remain vulnerable to various attack vectors, including:\n- Direct jailbreak attempts through crafted prompts\n- Indirect attacks exploiting code execution capabilities\n- Multi-turn conversation attacks that progressively elicit unsafe behavior\n- Cross-agent attacks exploiting differences in safety guardrails\n\nTheir experimental results highlighted significant vulnerabilities in existing code agent systems, emphasizing the need for robust defense mechanisms and comprehensive evaluation protocols.\n\n### 2.4 Blue-Teaming and Defense Strategies\n\nComplementing red-teaming efforts, defensive strategies for code agents have also been explored. Guo et al. developed \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI\" [4], which focuses on the defensive side of the security equation. This work introduces a blue-teaming agent that leverages insights from automated red-teaming to develop and test defensive strategies for code generation AI systems.\n\nBlueCodeAgent operates by:\n1. Learning from red-teaming results to understand attack patterns\n2. Generating defensive prompts and safety mechanisms\n3. Testing defensive strategies against adversarial inputs\n4. Iteratively improving defense capabilities based on evaluation outcomes\n\nThe synergistic relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming creates a feedback loop that continuously improves the security posture of code agent systems. BlueCodeAgent demonstrates that automated defense mechanisms can significantly reduce the success rate of adversarial attacks while maintaining the functionality and utility of code agents.\n\n## 3. Discussion\n\nThe research landscape for code agent security reveals several important trends and challenges:\n\n**Automation is Essential:** Manual red-teaming approaches are insufficient given the complexity and scope of potential attack vectors in code agents. Automated frameworks like RedCodeAgent and BlueCodeAgent provide scalable solutions for continuous security evaluation.\n\n**Comprehensive Evaluation is Critical:** The multi-dimensional approach to trustworthiness established by DecodingTrust reminds us that security is just one aspect of AI system evaluation. Code agents must be assessed across multiple dimensions including functionality, safety, fairness, and reliability.\n\n**The Role of Transformer Architecture:** The foundational work on attention mechanisms continues to influence code agent development. Understanding the architectural foundations enables researchers to identify and address model-specific vulnerabilities.\n\n**Red-Blue Team Dynamics:** The interplay between offensive (red teaming) and defensive (blue teaming) evaluation strategies creates a comprehensive security assessment framework. This dual approach ensures that both vulnerabilities and defenses are rigorously evaluated.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent a significant advancement in AI-assisted software development, but they also introduce novel security challenges that must be addressed before widespread deployment. This paper has reviewed key research advances in the field, highlighting the importance of automated red-teaming frameworks like RedCodeAgent and complementary defense strategies embodied by BlueCodeAgent. The foundational work on trustworthiness assessment through DecodingTrust and the transformer architecture introduced in \"Attention Is All You Need\" provide essential context for understanding and addressing code agent security concerns.\n\nFuture research should focus on developing standardized evaluation benchmarks for code agent security, creating more sophisticated attack and defense mechanisms, and establishing best practices for the safe deployment of code agents in production environments. The continuous evolution of both offensive and defensive techniques will be crucial in ensuring that code agents can deliver their transformative potential while maintaining robust security guarantees.\n\n## References\n\n[1] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[2] B. Wang, W. Chen, H. Pei et al., \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models,\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[3] C. Guo, C. Xie, Y. Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[4] C. Guo, Y. Nie, C. Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat /app/paper.txt | head -100"
}'}

